        --max-assumption-free 441
    platform-gated tests: 1080 in 249 files
    platform-conditional #[ignore] (exists there, skipped there): 26
    gated and also #[ignore]d everywhere: 0
    gated with no platform assumption in the body: 441 in 99 files
    would compile on:  linux 988  macos 850  other-unix 809  windows 62  other 29
    files with gated tests and no test that runs on the platform:  windows 74
    rc=0

    The same command run half an hour earlier was red with four problems:

      FAIL: unlisted platform-gated test .../lsp/manager.rs:829 a_unix_header_is_the_unix_path
      FAIL: unlisted platform-gated test .../lsp/manager.rs:839 a_windows_header_names_a_windows_path
      FAIL: unlisted platform-gated test .../lsp/tests.rs:2254 a_windows_document_uri_names_the_windows_path
      FAIL: 442 gated tests show no platform assumption in their body, over the budget of 441

    Those three tests were the regression tests written for the Windows LSP fix in
    section 10, and the first version of them was platform-gated. Raising the two
    budgets was not an option (they are ratchets), so the tests were rewritten to be
    platform-portable instead: a URI is decoded into this host's own path spelling,
    asserted through MAIN_SEPARATOR and Path::file_name rather than a literal that
    only one OS can check. The Windows drive-letter case did not lose coverage -- the
    ungated round-trip test builds its cases from a real temp path, so on Windows it
    compares `C:\...` against what hand-stripping returns (`\C:\...`) and fails there.
    Gating a test takes it away from the very platform whose bug it documents; the
    gate is what said so, in its own vocabulary, on the day it was written.

