== 10. The Windows LSP defect this review turned into a fix

    Symptom: run 37141224569 on origin/main had every job green except
    `platform tests (windows-latest)`, step `cargo test (target-OS crates:
    xai-grok-tools)`: 3111 passed, 3 failed. The three failures were
      an_answer_about_the_previous_revision_does_not_settle_the_new_one
      an_answer_the_store_refused_leaves_no_result_id_behind
      e2e_restart_replay_requeues_pending_diagnostics

    Root cause 1 (product): `replay_tracked_documents` turned a stored document URI
    back into a path with `strip_prefix("file://")`. Every URI it is handed came out
    of `file_uri`, so on Windows `file:///C:/dir/file.ts` became `/C:/dir/file.ts`,
    which resolves under the root of the current drive, and on every platform a name
    with a space kept its `%20`. `read_to_string` failed, `?` dropped the document,
    and a restarted server was silently told about none of the files it had been
    serving. `CollectedDiagnostics::append_file` built its header the same way, so
    the reader of a diagnostics summary was handed `/C:/dir/file.ts` or `%20` too.
    Fixed by one helper, `path_for_file_uri`, which parses and decodes.

    Root cause 2 (fixture): two Python mocks did `uri[len("file://"):]` and then
    wrote their readiness marker into `dirname` of that result. On Windows the marker
    landed outside the workspace, so the test waited out its budget and reported only
    that a marker never appeared. Fixed by `local_path()` / `touch_beside()` in the
    shared mock preamble, and one of the two tests now keeps its document inside a
    directory whose name needs escaping, so this fixture bug fails on Linux too
    instead of waiting for a Windows runner.

    Mutation matrix (each mutation applied, one named test observed, file restored and
    compared byte for byte; `cmp` after every single one):

      KILLED    W1 restart replay hand-strips file:// instead of decoding it
                a_replayed_document_whose_name_needs_escaping_reaches_the_new_server failed
      KILLED    W2 path_for_file_uri hand-strips instead of parsing
                a_file_uri_decodes_back_to_the_path_it_was_made_from failed
      KILLED    W3 diagnostics header cuts the scheme off the URI
                a_header_decodes_the_uri_instead_of_cutting_the_scheme_off failed
      KILLED    W4 mock server slices the URI instead of decoding it
                an_answer_about_the_previous_revision_does_not_settle_the_new_one failed
      SURVIVED  W5 mock server hides the fact that it could not write its marker

    W5 alone is an inert mutation, not a coverage gap: it deletes the `raise` inside
    `except OSError`, and with a correctly decoded URI that branch is never taken. A
    driver that cannot tell "the test failed" from "the branch never ran" would have
    reported it as a hole in the tests. What actually matters was measured instead:

      KILLED    W4  sliced URI, mock raises
                mock stderr line in output: False
                panicked at tests.rs:34: timed out waiting for the server to start
                its first pull
      KILLED    W5a sliced URI, mock swallows the OSError
                mock stderr line in output: False
                panicked at tests.rs:34: timed out waiting for the server to start
                its first pull

    Two facts follow. Detection does not depend on the `raise`: the test fails either
    way, with the same message. And the mock's stderr line reaches the test output in
    neither case -- `ServerStderr` only quotes the tail when a startup fails -- so the
    comment that claimed "the mock says so" was wrong about the payoff and was
    rewritten to say what is true: the mock dies because a mock that cannot locate
    its own document must not go on answering for it, and the test's own wait is what
    reports the failure.

    After the fix, on this host:

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib lsp::
    test result: ok. 128 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored; 0 measured; 3077 filtered out
    rc=0

    W3 was re-run after the header test was rewritten to be platform-portable:
      KILLED implementations::lsp::manager::tests::a_header_decodes_the_uri_instead_of_cutting_the_scheme_off
    restored byte for byte: True

== 11. The recon record, run for real for the first time

    `scripts/upstream-recon.sh` had only ever been exercised by its own fixtures. It
    ran against the real repository today (section 12 explains how) and behaved as
    designed:

      upstream-recon: sync/recon/2026-10-03-2bdd1d6a6.md already existed with
      different content; a recon record is never overwritten, so this one went to
      sync/recon/2026-10-03-2bdd1d6a6-2.md

    The stray file was deleted; nothing tracked was touched. The same run showed the
    record landing as 0600, because `mktemp` creates it private and `cp` carries that
    mode onto a new file. A recon record is a document other maintainers read, so the
    script now does `chmod 644` after placing it, and the fixture asserts it:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-upstream-recon.py
    Ran 10 tests in 0.865s ... OK

    Mutation, restored and compared byte for byte:
      rc with chmod removed: 1
      AssertionError: 0 != 36 : 0o600: a record other maintainers must read was
      written private
      RESTORED True
      rc after restore: 0

== 12. How this review was wrong before it was right

    These are recorded because each one produced either a misleading exit code or a
    side effect, and a reader of the numbers above should know which ones were
    re-taken.

    a) Two commands were invoked without their required argument and still exited 0
       because the pipeline ended in `tail`:

         $ python3 scripts/ci/ignored-tests.py --check-baseline | tail -3
         ignored-tests.py: error: argument --check-baseline: expected one argument
         rc=0
         $ python3 scripts/ci/classify-open-todos.py --check-doc | tail -3
         classify-open-todos.py: error: argument --check-doc: expected one argument
         rc=0

       argparse exits 2, `tail` exits 0, and the measurement script reported rc=0 for
       every line it ran. Both were re-run with their paths (sections 6 and 8). The
       general lesson: a gate runner that prints the exit code of the last stage of a
       pipeline is not reporting the gate's verdict.

    b) `git rev-list --count SOURCE_REV..upstream/main` failed: SOURCE_REV is a file
       name, not a revision. Correct form is
       `git rev-list --count "$(cat SOURCE_REV)..upstream/main"`.

    c) The worst one: the measurement script ran `bash scripts/ci/test-upstream-recon.py`
       instead of `python3 ...`. Bash executed the Python source as shell, which
       reached a line that calls the real `scripts/upstream-recon.sh` against the real
       repository and the real GitHub API. It created one untracked file,
       `sync/recon/2026-10-03-2bdd1d6a6-2.md`; nothing tracked was modified and the
       file was removed the same session. Two things are worth keeping from the
       accident: a fixture file is not a script to be handed to an arbitrary
       interpreter, and a harness line that begins `bash <something>.py` is a defect
       the reader should not have to discover by outcome. The review document cites
       that file name in an inline code span, so it is recorded in
       `scripts/ci/doc-path-refs-allowlist.tsv` as `recorded-absent`: the path gate
       cannot tell a deleted run artifact from a pointer that rotted, and the citing
       sentence is the place that says which one it is.

    d) `gh api .../actions/jobs/<id>/logs` earlier in this work returned nothing until
       `--allow-escape-sequences` was added, and `gh` only expands `:owner/:repo` when
       run inside the repository.

    e) One mutation in the earlier recon matrix reported SURVIVED for a reason that was
       a driver bug, not a coverage gap: mutating `test -n "$tip"` to
       `test -n "${tip-yes}"` is inert, because the `-` form only substitutes for an
       unset variable and `tip=$(...)` always sets it. Replaced with a mutation that
       changes behaviour (`true || {`), which was killed.

